⟦2500⟧ '''SOLANACEAE,''' in botany, an order of Dicotyledons belonging to the sub-class Sympetalae (or Gamopetalae) and to the series Tubiflorae, containing 75 genera with about 1500 species, widely distributed through the tropics, but passing into the temperate zones. The chief centre of the order lies in Central and South America; 32 of the genera are endemic in this region. It is represented in Britain by three genera including 4 species: ''Hyoscyamus niger'' (henbane), ''Solanum Dulcamara'' (Bittersweet) and ''S. nigrum'' and ''Atropa Belladonna'' (Deadly Nightshade).

⟦2900⟧ {{smaller block|The plants are herbs, shrubs or small trees. ''Solanum nigrum'', a common weed in waste places, is a low-growing annual herb; ''S. Dulcamara'' is an irregularly climbing herb perennial by means of a widely creeping rhizome; ''Atropa Belladonna'' is a large perennial herb. The genus ''Solanum'', to which belong more than half the number of species in the order, contains plants of very various habits including besides herbs, shrubs and trees. The leaves are generally alternate, but in the flower-bearing parts of the stem are}}

